1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
We found their tools.

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:03,080
Where are their clothes?

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:04,880
Picture a riverbank.

4
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:06,800
More than a million years ago,

5
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,120
someone picks up a stone
and chips away at it

6
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:11,360
until one edge becomes sharp.

7
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:14,000
Water, wind and time will wear away

8
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,920
almost everything around it.

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,400
But that stone will survive.

10
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,480
Thousands of generations pass.

11
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,000
New people come to the river,

12
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:23,680
bringing new tools with them:

13
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,960
bone needles, shaped
by careful human hands.

14
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,080
Stone and bone outlast
the people who made them.

15
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,400
Almost all their clothing is gone.

16
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:34,400
But not quite everything.

17
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:35,840
Here’s a tiny sandal,

18
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,680
woven from esparto grass.

19
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:39,840
A living person once wore it.

20
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,920
Imagine them pulling the straps tight,

21
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,040
stepping outside,

22
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:45,760
and heading off to whatever
the day had in store.

23
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,200
Thousands of years later,

24
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:49,120
the sandal is still here.

25
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,880
Suddenly, the past has more than tools.

26
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,120
It has feet.

27
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:54,960
Follow its owner's footsteps,

28
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,600
and we reach the other
bank: Roman Britain.

29
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:01,080
Tunics, cloaks, belts, shoes.

30
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:03,520
Now we're not looking at isolated traces.

31
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:05,040
We're looking at people,

32
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:07,600
dressed and ready to go about their lives.

33
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,440
One look gives way to another,

34
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,440
and the river carries us on.

35
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:12,960
By around 1400,

36
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,960
the silhouettes have changed again.

37
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:18,000
Long garments, belts, head coverings.

38
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:21,000
Straight lines give way
to folds and layers.

39
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:22,120
Even from a distance,

40
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,880
you can see how different
a passerby looks.

41
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:28,040
Now it's Amsterdam, 1559.

42
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,560
A family portrait.

43
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:30,720
Look closely:

44
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:33,040
these people belong to the same family,

45
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,160
but each has a style of their own.

46
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,280
Different hats, fabrics, little details.

47
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:39,147
One painting,

48
00:01:39,173 --> 00:01:40,800
and a whole family of strangers

49
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,400
looking straight at us
across the centuries.

50
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:44,920
Which would you choose?

51
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:47,720
The river carries us into
the eighteenth century.

52
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,880
A French dress spreads wide.

53
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,160
Beside it, a British court outfit:

54
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:55,653
fine decoration, a waistcoat, a long coat.

55
00:01:55,707 --> 00:01:57,600
You wouldn't exactly
blend into the background

56
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,160
wearing either of these.

57
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,293
Walk into a room,

58
00:02:00,347 --> 00:02:02,680
and your outfit arrives before you do.

59
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:04,920
But wide is not the limit.

60
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,213
In the nineteenth century,

61
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:08,560
the crinoline appears beneath the skirt.

62
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,240
Suddenly, a person is surrounded

63
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,480
by a whole dome of fabric.

64
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:14,373
Imagine trying to get through a doorway,

65
00:02:14,427 --> 00:02:16,600
or sitting next to someone in a carriage.

66
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:18,293
Now let's jump ahead.

67
00:02:18,347 --> 00:02:19,893
The 1920s.

68
00:02:19,947 --> 00:02:20,933
In some dresses,

69
00:02:20,987 --> 00:02:22,560
that enormous dome gives way

70
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:24,640
to a completely different shape:

71
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,000
long, straight and loose.

72
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,640
Like a broad wave on the river

73
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,360
suddenly smoothing out.

74
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,333
One more step,

75
00:02:31,387 --> 00:02:33,200
and we're on a modern street.

76
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:34,520
Jeans and dresses,

77
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,360
sneakers and tailored suits,

78
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:37,680
all side by side.

79
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:39,840
We wear these things every day,

80
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:41,360
hardly thinking that one day

81
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:42,880
they'll be history too.

82
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,080
Picture a museum in the future.

83
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,560
Behind the glass:

84
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:50,040
our sneakers, a jacket,
someone's favorite T-shirt.

85
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,080
Visitors study them as closely

86
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,000
as we study that ancient sandal.

87
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,360
Maybe they wonder who wore
them, and where they were going.

88
00:02:57,920 --> 00:02:59,440
The river keeps flowing.

89
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:01,840
Things survive, disappear,

90
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:04,120
and surprise people centuries later.

91
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:05,760
We found their tools.

92
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,600
What will they find after us?

